<section id="research-highlights" class="content-section"></section>
  <div class="container">
    <div class="title-bar-index pad-content">
      <h2>
        research highlights
      </h2>
      <div class="row">
        <div class="col-md-12">
          <p>
            <b>I am broadly interested in creating novel interfaces for emerging computing technologies that enable users to think and solve problems faster or in new ways.</b>
          </p>
          <p>
            I am especially interested in XR/spatial computing systems like virtual and augmented reality (VR/AR).
            As these devices transition into general purpose computers that are used more frequently, for longer periods, and for more applications, it is important that they provide an efficient and seamless experience for interactions with digital content and with other people across different contexts.
            Our current personal computers (laptops, tablets, smartphones) provide features for quickly switching between different applications and workspaces that we regularly use and enjoy, such as Alt+Tab/Cmd+Tab and touchpad swipe gestures.
            {{!-- This seamless switching between digital contexts is now a regular part of how we use computers. --}}
            However, the current process for switching between spatial computing environments is a far cry from the fast and seamless switching experience we have on our other devices.
            Creating this seamless switching experience is a challenging user interface problem because spatial applications typically involve whole worlds, not just 2D windows in a stable frame, and so smoothly interacting across them or switching between them is more complex.
            For instance, after switching to a new spatial environment, the user must orient themselves (i.e., understand where they are with respect to the environment and objects within it) before they can complete their first intended action there.
          </p>
          {{!-- <p>
            To address this fundamental usability problem, my PhD research focuses on creating effective cross-reality interfaces, interactions, and transitions.
            The concept of "cross-reality" is most easily illustrated using Milgram and Kishino's Reality-Virtuality (RV) Continuum, a seminal piece of virtual reality (VR) research that introduced the figure below to classify immersive virtual systems.
            On one end of the continuum is the physical world with no virtual content displayed in it.
            On the other end is a fully virtual environment (i.e., VR).
            These extremes are set on a continuum because they can be mixed in interesting ways.
            If your primary environment is the physical world, and you add virtual content to it, you get augmented reality (AR) (think: Pokemon Go).
            If your primary environment is a virtual world, and you add elements of the physical environment to it, you get augmented virtuality (AV) (imagine being in a VR headset and seeing real-time video of the physical world through a portal).
          </p>--}}
          {{!-- <img class="proj-thumbnail center" src="images/RV-continuum-2.jpg" alt="Milgram and Kishino's Reality-Virtuality Continuum" max-width="100%"></img>  --}}
          <img class="proj-thumbnail center" src="images/worldswitch_timeline.png" alt="World Switch Timeline" max-width="100%"></img> 
          <p class="break-above">
            To address this fundamental usability problem space, my PhD research focuses on creating effective world switching interfaces and cross-reality interactions, as well as studying the cognitive effects of such interfaces and transitions.
{{!-- 
            In my research, I have explored methods for efficiently and effectively transitioning a single user between different virtual environments.
            I have also investigated ways to improve interactions between people on opposite ends of the continuum, specifically focusing on when an immersed VR user is interrupted by a non-immersed person nearby in the user's physical environment. --}}
          </p>
          <h5>
            Some of my research projects in this area:
          </h5>
        </div>
      </div>

