        {{!-- <div class="col-xs-9 col-md-9 proj-group-details"> --}}
        <div class="col-9 proj-group-details">
          <details id="worldSwitchUI_Details">
          <summary>
              <span class="summary-title">
                World Switching User Interfaces
              </span>
              <h6 class="subtitle subtitle-break">
                Structured design exploration of interfaces for efficiently selecting and transitioning between virtual environments
              </h6>
          </summary>
          <div class="card card-body infoItem--card">
            <p>
              [ <a href="docs/papers/CHI26__Paper_worldswitchUI.pdf">Paper PDF</a> ]
              [ <a href="https://dl.acm.org/doi/abs/10.1145/3772318.3791912">ACM DL</a> ]
              [ <a href="https://github.com/mott-lab/WorldSwitchUI/">Code + Demo</a> ]
              [ <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VOiOCIRqqNA">Video</a> ]  
              [ <a href="https://scholar.google.com/citations?view_op=view_citation&hl=en&user=lHM2NP4AAAAJ&citation_for_view=lHM2NP4AAAAJ:_kc_bZDykSQC">Google Scholar</a> ]
              [ <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.01423">arXiv</a> ]
            </p>
            <p>
              This project started with a couple simple, fundamental questions: why do we use VR apps strictly in a "one-world-at-a-time" mode of operation? What would it look like to create interfaces that let us switch between VR apps in a way similar to how we quickly switch between apps on desktop computers? Coming up with satisfying solutions was a little more complicated than it looked at first glance due to the spatial complexity of VR environments ("worlds"). 
            </p>
            <p>
              Desktop switching interfaces like Alt+Tab or task views provide application previews for quickly getting the gist of an application, as well as a continuous interaction flow. These core features enable users to complete fast switches. However, since VR can display content all around the user and not just within a bounded 2D frame, world switching previews need to represent spatial information. This requirement adds interaction complexity: when a spatial preview is used in a world switching interface, a continuous interaction flow needs to support both efficient world selection and preview manipulation.
            </p>
            <p>
              We created an interaction model to break down the world switching process:
            </p>
            <div class="center">
              <img src="images/worldswitchUI/World_Switch_UI_Flow-10.png" width="80%">
            </div>
            <br>
            <p>
              We considered two spatial previews in our interface design exploration: <b>world-in-miniature (WiM)</b>, which represents a scaled-down 3D overview representation of a world; and <b>portal</b>, which provides a first-person view into a world through a window.
            </p>
            <p>
              As preview interactions are inherently embodied, we decided to focus our interaction technique design on body-based reference frames (the spatial coordinate systems relative to which the interface's virtual content and interactions are defined). To keep this first exploration focused, we considered three user-centered reference frames common in 3DUI interaction techniques: one hand, both hands, and head. You can check <a href="docs/papers/CHI26__Paper_worldswitchUI.pdf">the paper</a> for our full design process, but we arrived at four interaction technique designs, which we combined with each preview type for a total of 8 interfaces. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VOiOCIRqqNA">Check out the video to see our interfaces in action</a>.
            </p>
            {{!-- <div class="center">
              <img src="images/worldswitchUI/worldswitch_uis.png" width="100%">
            </div> --}}
            {{!-- <br> --}}
            <p>
              We ran a user study with a simple coin collection task across several similarly structured environments. We compared all 8 interfaces and a baseline interface that consisted of a 2D grid of world options without previews anda ray-based selection interaction technique. We measured how fast users were able to <b>search</b> for and find the coin, <b>retrieve</b> it, and <b>deposit</b> it in the "home" environment. This allowed us to understand the overall efficiency of our interfaces, the pre-orientation abilities of each preview type, and the efficiency of the interaction techniques for performing familiar interactions, respectively. We also used subjective questionnaires and interviewed participants to gain insight into user experience.
            </p>
            <div >
              <img class="center" src="images/worldswitchUI/box_search_tech_4.png" width="50%">
            </div>
            <br>
            <p>
              We found that for our task, interfaces with WiMs generally provided the fastest search times due to providing an effective overview that enabled quickly finding which world had the search target.
            </p>
            <div >
              <img class="center" src="images/worldswitchUI/box_retrieve_deposit_5.png" width="50%">
            </div>
            <br>
            <p>
              The portal outperformed both the WiM and baseline in terms of pre-orientation. Our gallery technique (which involved a linear scroll) performed the slowest for the return-to-home interaction.
            </p>
            <p>
              If you want to try out the interfaces, you can <a href="https://github.com/mott-lab/WorldSwitchUI/releases/tag/v0.1">download a Windows .exe</a> to run a demo on a computer connected to a VR headset. If you want to hack on our interfaces, check out <a href="https://github.com/mott-lab/WorldSwitchUI">the repo</a> as well. <a href="#contact">Let me know</a> if you try them out or have any questions!
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
        </div>
      </div>
      
