        {{!-- <div class="col-xs-9 col-md-9 proj-group-details"> --}}
        <div class="col-9 proj-group-details">
          <details id="augmentedPresentationsDetails">
          <summary>
              <span class="summary-title">
                Augmented Presentations
              </span>

              <h6 class="subtitle subtitle-break">
                Investigating the effects of asymmetric interfaces on engagement and social interaction in co-located augmented presentations
              </h6>
          </summary>
          <div class="card card-body infoItem--card">
            <p>
              [ <a href="/docs/papers/CHI25__Paper__Immersive_Presentation_Study_preprint.pdf">Paper PDF</a> ]
              [ <a href="https://dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/3706598.3713346">ACM DL</a> ]
              [ <a href="https://scholar.google.com/citations?view_op=view_citation&hl=en&user=lHM2NP4AAAAJ&sortby=pubdate&citation_for_view=lHM2NP4AAAAJ:MXK_kJrjxJIC">Google Scholar</a> ]
            </p>
            <p>
              In this study, we explored using augmented reality (AR) to provide an engaging, immersive experience for one-on-one co-located presentations. Think: financial planning meetings of the future. In designing this experience, head-worn AR seemed like a good fit for the audience so they can be fully immersed in the presentation content --- they would be able to see and interact with 3D data visualizations displayed in the room around them. 
            </p>
            <p>

But what about for the presenter? Imagine if you have to give many presentations back-to-back, without much of a break. You would likely get fatigued wearing current state-of-the-art AR headsets --- they simply are not comfortable enough for extended use. Additionally, the headset covers the top half of the presenter's face, which blocks their facial expressions from the audience's view and could hamper social engagement.

For these reasons, we built and tested two versions of our immersive presentation system prototype: one in which both the audience and presenter used AR headsets (symmetric), and one in which the presenter used a tablet to control the audience's AR experience (asymmetric).
</p>

<p>

We found some trade-offs: The asymmetric version provided the presenter with good control and direction of the presentation, while the symmetric version felt more like a collaborative experience between participants. The asymmetric system supported good social connection between the presenter and audience through typical social behaviors, but felt less like a shared experience. The symmetric system provided a spatial common ground and thus supported intuitive, direct referencing. We recommend weighing these findings when designing a presentation system based on the desired presenter/audience experience.
</p>

<p>

We also recommend providing familiar and conventional presentation interfaces, integrating both 2D and 3D content in immersive presentations, as well as enabling both pre-planned presentation content and real-time adjustments. 
</p>
<p>
Read more in our paper, Examining the Effects of Immersive and Non-Immersive Presenter Modalities on Engagement and Social Interaction in Co-located Augmented Presentations.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="row proj-summary-group">
